0345: SapphireGpu: GPU core clock changed at run time (kern.sweet_gpu_khz, kern.sweet_gpu_opp)

Nothing changes unless kern.sweet_gpu_khz is written. Needs 0337's stock path (gpu_vco2=1 with a gpu_mhz of
the stock table) and 0343.

kern.sweet_gpu_khz (int, read-write): reads the core clock; writing a row of the stock table (kHz, or MHz)
moves the clock there, not above the speed bin's top (gpu_anybin=1 lifts it), with the votes kgsl makes:
- up: CX peak-current votes from 900 and 950 MHz, the CX rail by the rate and the MX rail by the VCO
  (never under 0337's 0x1a0 unless gpu_cx/gpu_mx say so), then the clock; down: the clock, then the rails,
  then the peak-current votes the new rate does not need (stock clk_pre/post_change_regmap order);
- the clock: with service_lock held and the GPU idle (ring read to its end, RBBM_STATUS idle; boot-arg
  gpu_opp_drain=N ms, 100), gpu_cc_pll0 takes its new L and alpha while running, as the stock
  clk_zonda_pll_set_rate() does for a PLL that is on (SUPPORTS_DYNAMIC_UPDATE), then waits for the lock
  bit; going down L is written first, so that between the two writes the PLL never aims above the higher
  of the two rates;
- kern.sweet_gpu_opp_park=1 (boot-arg gpu_opp_park=1): the RCG to GPLL0/2 (300 MHz) while the PLL is
  restarted at the new rate, then back.
Errors: EINVAL (not a row, above the bin), ENODEV (no stock path, no service, failed recovery), EBUSY (GPU
not idle in time; votes taken back), EAGAIN (peak-current limit refused), EIO (rail vote refused, or no PLL
lock). A PLL without lock within 1 ms is brought back the long way at its old rate; if that fails, the core
stays on GPLL0/2 and later changes are refused; if the RCG does not switch, the GPU service is stopped.

kern.sweet_gpu_opp (read only): rate, mode, changes, refusals by cause, PLL failures and recoveries, the last
and worst changes with their times, the votes in place, time at each row.

Locking: opp_lock serialises changes and is taken before service_lock; RPM and peak-current votes run under
opp_lock only.

Checked on the host against a model of the Zonda PLL, the RCG, the GPU, the RPM and cx_ipeak (every step of
the table both ways and both modes, busy GPU, refused votes, no lock, stuck RCG): the core clock stays within
the voted CX level and the VCO within the MX level at every register write.
Measured on the phone (n=1): 820 -> 1025 -> 1260 MHz without errors, 312 and 1344 us per change; gxfreq
reads 1025.00 and 1260.00 MHz.

